From: Magnus Lindholm <linmag7@gmail.com>
Subject: [RFC PATCH 0/3] sunsu receive status,
 panic locking and UART handoff
MIME-Version: 1.0
Content-Type: text/plain; charset=UTF-8
Content-Transfer-Encoding: 8bit

Preserve receive error status consumed by transmit polling, avoid waiting
for the console port lock during panic, and provide explicit UART handoff
for firmware entry. The caller pins the console and serial configuration,
then transfers register ownership without holding a port lock in firmware.

The handoff saves and verifies register state and excludes native console
MMIO while firmware owns the UART. Exceptional contexts refuse handoff.
A caller must contain restoration failure rather than resume with unknown
state. PC16550D SNLS378C sections 8.6.2-8.6.6 describe these registers.

This group applies independently to vanilla v7.3-rc1. Earlier PRIMEPOWER
hardware tests exercised serial, BREAK/go and native console traffic. A
RAM-only held-port panic fixture observed twelve skipped writes and zero
UART MMIO. The simulated firmware-owned panic case produced the same
result. External recovery and exact cleaned UP/SMP boot, native serial and
BREAK tests passed. Neither fixture claims cancellation of an actual
in-flight firmware call.

On Ultra45, the exact cleaned integration passed SUNSU/ST16650 serial
receive/transmit, transmit-status polling, BREAK/go and warm reboot.
Paced input passed alongside console output. Input bursts during printk
overran the non-FIFO receive path on both the old kernel and candidate;
this series does not make that workload lossless. No framing/parity
error injection or non-PRIMEPOWER UART ownership handoff is claimed.

Magnus Lindholm:
  serial: sunsu: preserve receive errors across transmit polling
  serial: sunsu: avoid waiting for a port lock during panic
  serial: sunsu: hand the console to firmware explicitly

